+      "logical_name": "gaussian-intermediate-level-tail-relative-log-correction",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Relative log correction in a Gaussian intermediate-level tail",
+      "statement": "Fix g>0, lambda>0 and a bounded interval of B. For L=log N and H centered Gaussian with variance g(L+B), as L->infinity uniformly over B, P(H>=2sqrt(g)lambda L)=N^{-2lambda^2}exp(2lambda^2 B)/(2lambda sqrt(2pi L)) times [1+(B/2-2lambda^2 B^2-1/(4lambda^2))/L+O(L^{-2})]. The variance remainder B is its exact finite-N value. This is a one-point statement, not a level-set fluctuation theorem.",
+      "proof_text": "# C0002: a uniform bulk one-point tail expansion\n\nStatus: proof of a one-point expansion, awaiting w346. This continues the corrected Gaussian-tail normalization. It does not identify the fluctuation law or large deviations of the entire DGFF level set.\n\nFix g>0 and lambda>0. Set L=log N and let a centered Gaussian H have variance gL+b, with B=b/g in a fixed bounded interval. For L sufficiently large,\n\n    P(H >= 2 sqrt(g) lambda L)\n      = N^{-2lambda^2} exp(2lambda^2 B)/(2lambda sqrt(2pi L))\n        * [1 + (B/2 - 2lambda^2 B^2 - 1/(4lambda^2))/L + O(L^{-2})],\n\nuniformly over that interval of B. The variance is assumed to be exactly gL+b; for a varying finite-N variance, b can be its exact remainder. A limiting b alone is insufficient for the displayed correction without controlling its finite-N error.\n\nProof: with x=2lambda L/sqrt(L+B), Mills' expansion is Phi_bar(x)=exp(-x^2/2)/(sqrt(2pi)x) * (1-x^{-2}+O(x^{-4})). It follows by repeated integration by parts, with the next integral bounding the remainder uniformly when x tends to infinity. Expand\n\n    x^2/2=2lambda^2 L-2lambda^2 B+2lambda^2 B^2/L+O(L^{-2}),\n    1/x=(2lambda sqrt(L))^{-1}[1+B/(2L)+O(L^{-2})],\n    1/x^2=1/(4lambda^2 L)+O(L^{-2}),\n\nand multiply. Boundedness of B makes every remainder uniform.\n\nThus bounded variance corrections enter the LEADING factor exp(2lambda^2 b/g), not merely a vanishing additive correction. On a bulk set of DGFF vertices where b_N(x)=Var(h_x)-g log N is uniformly bounded, linearity of expectation permits summing this expansion with B=b_N(x)/g. No independence of vertices is needed for the expectation. Pair correlations and the scale ladder remain necessary for fluctuation laws.\n\nSource: C0002-A002 in the cached intake, which corrected the missing (log N)^{-1/2} leading normalization. The explicit relative 1/log N term here is independently derived campaign work.\n",
